{
  "explanation": "Round-001 established the first verified baseline on F_competition: a bottleneck-priority rule produced a complete, state-valid, independently replayed batch of 4 wafers with makespan 74.0 s, zero constraint violations, and zero deadlock. This is a valid incumbent but not proven optimal. The nominal RINSE lower bound is 28.0 s, so 74.0 s leaves a large gap (46.0 s) attributable to robot action time (45.0 s), blocking (42.0 s total, with CLEAN 23.0 s and ALT 13.0 s), and waiting (average 23.75 s). Notably, RINSE blocking is only 6.0 s while CLEAN/ALT blocking is 36.0 s combined, suggesting the bottleneck rule over-prioritizes RINSE and starves upstream chambers, causing them to block. The bottleneck rule is therefore not obviously the best rule; a different dispatch policy or a CP-SAT search may find a shorter verified makespan. Since no CP-SAT or RL result exists yet, the next cheapest informative probe is a CP-SAT run at the smallest allowed budget (1 s) on the same scenario, which can either match or beat 74.0 s and will reveal whether the rule is near-optimal or far from it.",
  "failure_explanation": "No failure occurred in round-001: the experiment completed, verification passed, and the candidate was accepted as the first verified incumbent. The only limitation is that the result is a single rule-based point estimate with no optimality certificate, so it cannot yet be claimed optimal or near-optimal.",
  "testable_hypothesis": "On F_competition, a CP-SAT search with max_seconds=1 will return a verified complete-batch Makespan strictly less than 74.0 s (the round-001 bottleneck-rule incumbent), because the observed 42.0 s of blocking and 45.0 s of robot action time indicate substantial scheduling slack that a bottleneck-priority dispatch rule does not exploit.",
  "next_proposal": {
    "action": "experiment",
    "scenario_id": "F_competition",
    "method": "cpsat",
    "parameters": {
      "max_seconds": 1
    },
    "rationale": "Round-001 gives a verified rule incumbent of 74.0 s on F_competition but no optimality evidence. The smallest allowed CP-SAT budget (1 s) is the cheapest way to test whether the rule is near-optimal or whether search can improve it. This also begins mapping the CP-SAT budget/quality curve before spending larger budgets. The scenario has a single dominant bottleneck (RINSE nominal load 28.0 s), which is a favorable structure for exact search.",
    "hypothesis": "CP-SAT at 1 s will find a verified complete-batch Makespan below 74.0 s on F_competition, or return OPTIMAL at a value at or below 74.0 s, demonstrating that the bottleneck rule is not optimal.",
    "expected_effect": "Either a verified makespan < 74.0 s (rule suboptimal) or an OPTIMAL status at <= 74.0 s (rule near-optimal). Either outcome is informative for deciding whether to invest in larger CP budgets or in alternative rules/RL.",
    "evidence": [
      "round-001"
    ]
  }
}

{
  "explanation": "Round-002 tested the previous hypothesis that CP-SAT at max_seconds=1 would beat the round-001 bottleneck-rule incumbent of 74.0 s on F_competition. The hypothesis was confirmed: CP-SAT returned a verified complete-batch makespan of 63.0 s, an 11.0 s (14.9%) improvement, with independent replay valid, zero constraint violations, and zero deadlock. The solver status was FEASIBLE, not OPTIMAL, with a best bound of 51.0 s and an absolute gap of 12.0 s (relative gap 19.0%). This is important: the search did not prove optimality, so 63.0 s is a better incumbent but not a certified optimum. The nominal RINSE lower bound is 28.0 s, so a gap of at least 35.0 s remains between the incumbent and the nominal bottleneck load, and at least 12.0 s remains between the incumbent and the solver's own bound. The cost ratio to the rule incumbent is about 7.28x in tool seconds (1.73 s skill time vs 0.29 s rule time), so the improvement is real but not free. The result also shows the bottleneck rule was substantially suboptimal, consistent with the earlier observation that it over-prioritized RINSE and starved upstream chambers. The next cheapest informative probe is a larger CP-SAT budget on the same scenario to see whether the bound tightens and the incumbent improves further, which will reveal whether 63.0 s is near-optimal or whether more search is needed.",
  "failure_explanation": "No failure occurred in round-002: the experiment completed, verification passed, and the candidate was accepted as a lower-makespan incumbent. The only limitation is that the solver returned FEASIBLE rather than OPTIMAL, so optimality is not established and the 12.0 s gap to the best bound remains open. The 1 s budget was sufficient to improve the incumbent but not to close the gap.",
  "testable_hypothesis": "On F_competition, a CP-SAT search with max_seconds=3 will either return a verified complete-batch Makespan strictly below 63.0 s or return OPTIMAL at a value at or below 63.0 s, because the round-002 run at 1 s left a 12.0 s absolute gap (best bound 51.0 s) and 34890 branches explored, indicating that additional search time can either improve the incumbent or tighten the bound enough to certify optimality.",
  "next_proposal": {
    "action": "experiment",
    "scenario_id": "F_competition",
    "method": "cpsat",
    "parameters": {
      "max_seconds": 3
    },
    "rationale": "Round-002 improved the F_competition incumbent from 74.0 s to 63.0 s with CP-SAT at 1 s, but returned FEASIBLE with a 12.0 s gap to the best bound of 51.0 s. The next allowed CP budget (3 s) is the cheapest way to test whether the incumbent can be improved further or whether the bound can be tightened enough to certify optimality. This continues mapping the CP-SAT budget/quality curve on the scenario with a single dominant bottleneck (RINSE nominal load 28.0 s), which is favorable for exact search. The estimated experiment cost is about 4 s, well within the remaining wall budget and the 40 s per-experiment cap.",
    "hypothesis": "CP-SAT at 3 s will either find a verified complete-batch Makespan below 63.0 s on F_competition or return OPTIMAL at a value at or below 63.0 s, demonstrating that the 1 s budget was insufficient to close the gap.",
    "expected_effect": "Either a verified makespan < 63.0 s (further improvement) or an OPTIMAL status at <= 63.0 s (certified optimality), or a tighter best bound with FEASIBLE status. Any of these outcomes informs whether to invest in larger CP budgets, alternative rules, or RL on this scenario.",
    "evidence": [
      "round-001",
      "round-002"
    ]
  }
}

{
  "explanation": "Round-003 tested the previous hypothesis that CP-SAT at max_seconds=3 would either improve the F_competition incumbent below 63.0 s or return OPTIMAL at or below 63.0 s. The result falsified the improvement/optimality part of the hypothesis: the solver again returned FEASIBLE at exactly 63.0 s, with the same best bound of 51.0 s, the same 12.0 s absolute gap (19.0% relative), and the same verified makespan. The candidate was verified (independent replay valid, zero constraint violations, zero deadlock) but was not accepted because it produced no lexicographic improvement over the round-002 incumbent. This is a meaningful negative result: tripling the CP-SAT budget from 1 s to 3 s increased branches from 34890 to 37268 and conflicts from 5780 to 5780 (unchanged), yet the incumbent and bound did not move at all. That strongly suggests the 1 s run had already reached a local plateau for this model encoding, and that the remaining 12.0 s gap is not closable by simply adding more time to the same formulation. The verified metrics also reveal the structural reason: RINSE processing is 28.0 s (the nominal bottleneck), but chamber blocking is 45.0 s total (CLEAN 19.0, ALT 19.0, RINSE 7.0), and robot utilization is 0.698 during the completion window. The makespan of 63.0 s is therefore dominated by robot transfer serialization and chamber blocking, not by raw chamber processing load. The nominal RINSE lower bound of 28.0 s is far below the achieved 63.0 s, confirming that the bottleneck is the single-gripper robot schedule, not chamber capacity. Cost-wise, round-003 consumed about 4.28 s wall and 3.58 s skill time for zero improvement, a cost ratio of about 2.04x the incumbent's tool time, so further blind CP budget increases on this scenario are poor value. The next cheapest informative probe is a different search method on the same scenario: an RL policy evaluation using an allowed pretrained model, which explores a different region of the schedule space than the exact CP encoding and may escape the plateau. If RL also fails to beat 63.0 s, that would strengthen the case that 63.0 s is near-optimal for this instance and that effort should shift to the untried G_dual_bottleneck scenario.",
  "failure_explanation": "Round-003 did not fail verification: the simulation completed, independent replay was valid, and there were zero constraint violations and zero deadlocks. It failed only the acceptance criterion of lexicographic improvement, because the CP-SAT search at 3 s returned the same FEASIBLE makespan of 63.0 s and the same best bound of 51.0 s as the 1 s run. The hypothesis that additional CP search time would either improve the incumbent or certify optimality was falsified. The likely cause is that the CP model's search plateaued: the incumbent hint from round-002 was reused, branches increased only modestly (34890 to 37268), and the bound did not tighten, indicating the remaining gap is structural to the model encoding rather than a matter of search time. The 12.0 s gap to the bound therefore remains open and is not evidence that 63.0 s is optimal.",
  "testable_hypothesis": "On F_competition, an RL policy evaluation using an allowed pretrained model (medium-seed-0) will return a verified complete-batch Makespan different from 63.0 s, because the CP-SAT plateau at 63.0 s across 1 s and 3 s budgets indicates the exact encoding is stuck in a local region, and a learned policy explores a different schedule space; if RL also returns 63.0 s or worse, that would support treating 63.0 s as near-optimal for this instance and shifting effort to G_dual_bottleneck.",
  "next_proposal": {
    "action": "experiment",
    "scenario_id": "F_competition",
    "method": "rl",
    "parameters": {
      "model_id": "medium-seed-0"
    },
    "rationale": "Round-003 showed that tripling the CP-SAT budget from 1 s to 3 s produced no improvement and no bound tightening on F_competition, so further CP budget increases on the same encoding are poor value. The cheapest untried method on this scenario is RL policy evaluation with an allowed pretrained model. RL explores a different schedule space than the exact CP encoding and may escape the 63.0 s plateau. The estimated experiment cost is about 10 s, within the remaining wall budget and the 40 s per-experiment cap. This also begins mapping method performance on F_competition, which is needed before comparing tool cost across methods.",
    "hypothesis": "RL policy evaluation with medium-seed-0 on F_competition will return a verified complete-batch Makespan different from 63.0 s; if it is lower, the CP plateau is method-specific, and if it is equal or higher, 63.0 s is likely near-optimal for this instance.",
    "expected_effect": "Either a verified makespan below 63.0 s (new incumbent, showing RL escapes the CP plateau), or a verified makespan at or above 63.0 s (supporting near-optimality of 63.0 s and justifying a shift to G_dual_bottleneck). Either outcome informs the method comparison and the decision to stop investing in F_competition CP search.",
    "evidence": [
      "round-001",
      "round-002",
      "round-003"
    ]
  }
}

{
  "explanation": "Round-004 tested the hypothesis that RL policy evaluation with medium-seed-0 would return a verified complete-batch Makespan different from the 63.0 s CP-SAT incumbent on F_competition, potentially escaping the CP plateau. The result is a verified but non-improving candidate: makespan 65.0 s, which is 2.0 s worse than the incumbent, with independent replay valid, zero constraint violations, and zero deadlocks. The hypothesis of a different makespan is confirmed (65.0 s ≠ 63.0 s), but the directional expectation of improvement is falsified. The RL policy is therefore not better than the CP-SAT incumbent on this instance. The structural metrics explain why: RL produced 68.0 s of total chamber blocking (CLEAN 26.0, ALT 36.0, RINSE 6.0) versus the incumbent's 45.0 s, a 23.0 s increase in blocking, while robot utilization fell to 0.18 and mean chamber utilization to 0.048. The RL schedule serializes transfers poorly, leaving chambers blocked and wafers waiting (average waiting 25.75 s). This is consistent with the earlier interpretation that F_competition makespan is dominated by robot transfer serialization and chamber blocking rather than raw chamber processing load. Cost-wise, round-004 consumed about 4.68 s wall and 3.05 s skill time for zero improvement, a cost ratio of about 2.23x the incumbent's tool time, so RL at this model is also poor value on F_competition. Combined with round-003, we now have two independent methods (CP-SAT at 3 s and RL medium-seed-0) failing to beat 63.0 s, which strengthens the case that 63.0 s is near-optimal for this instance under the current encodings. The remaining untried CP budget is 10 s, but the 1 s and 3 s runs both returned the same FEASIBLE 63.0 s with the same 51.0 s bound, so a 10 s run is unlikely to close the 12.0 s gap and would cost more. The more informative next step is to begin mapping the untried G_dual_bottleneck scenario, which has no incumbent and a different nominal bottleneck (CLEAN, with CLEAN and RINSE both at 56.0 s nominal load). A cheap rule probe there establishes a baseline and tests whether the rule policies transfer across scenario structure. This also advances the cross-scenario method comparison that the objective requires, without spending more on a plateaued F_competition instance.",
  "failure_explanation": "Round-004 did not fail verification: the simulation completed, independent replay was valid, and there were zero constraint violations and zero deadlocks. It failed only the acceptance criterion of lexicographic improvement, because the RL policy returned a verified makespan of 65.0 s, which is 2.0 s worse than the 63.0 s incumbent. The hypothesis that RL would return a different makespan was confirmed, but the expectation that it might improve on the CP plateau was falsified. The likely cause is that the pretrained RL policy (medium-seed-0) was not trained to optimize this specific F_competition instance and produced a schedule with much higher chamber blocking (68.0 s vs 45.0 s) and lower robot utilization (0.18), indicating poor transfer serialization. This is a meaningful negative result: it shows the CP plateau at 63.0 s is not trivially escapable by a generic learned policy, and it supports treating 63.0 s as near-optimal for this instance under the tested methods.",
  "testable_hypothesis": "On G_dual_bottleneck, a cheap rule policy (bottleneck) will return a verified complete-batch Makespan that establishes a baseline incumbent, and its blocking-to-processing ratio and robot utilization will differ from F_competition's, because G_dual_bottleneck has a different nominal bottleneck (CLEAN, with CLEAN and RINSE both at 56.0 s nominal load) and zero blocking-to-processing ratio in the static analysis; if the rule baseline is far above the nominal 56.0 s lower bound, that would indicate robot serialization dominates there too, and if it is close, chamber capacity dominates.",
  "next_proposal": {
    "action": "experiment",
    "scenario_id": "G_dual_bottleneck",
    "method": "rule",
    "parameters": {
      "policy": "bottleneck"
    },
    "rationale": "F_competition now has two independent non-improving methods (CP-SAT at 3 s and RL medium-seed-0) against the 63.0 s incumbent, so further investment there is poor value. G_dual_bottleneck has no incumbent and a different nominal bottleneck (CLEAN, with CLEAN and RINSE both at 56.0 s nominal load), making it the most informative untried scenario. A rule probe with the bottleneck policy is the cheapest available method (sub-second tool time) and establishes a verified baseline for cross-scenario method comparison, which the objective requires. This also tests whether rule policies transfer across scenario structure before spending on CP or RL there.",
    "hypothesis": "The bottleneck rule policy on G_dual_bottleneck will return a verified complete-batch Makespan establishing a baseline incumbent; its blocking-to-processing ratio and robot utilization will differ from F_competition's, reflecting the different nominal bottleneck (CLEAN) and the zero static blocking-to-processing ratio.",
    "expected_effect": "A verified baseline makespan for G_dual_bottleneck, enabling comparison of rule performance across scenarios and informing whether CP-SAT or RL is worth trying there. If the baseline is far above the 56.0 s nominal lower bound, robot serialization likely dominates; if close, chamber capacity dominates.",
    "evidence": [
      "round-001",
      "round-002",
      "round-003",
      "round-004"
    ]
  }
}

{
  "explanation": "Round-005 completed the first probe of G_dual_bottleneck using the bottleneck rule policy. The result is verified: status completed, 4 of 4 wafers released and completed, zero unfinished wafers, independent replay valid, zero constraint violations, zero deadlocks. It establishes the first incumbent for this scenario at makespan 199.0 s, accepted as first_verified_candidate. This is a baseline, not an optimum. The static analysis predicted a nominal bottleneck of CLEAN with CLEAN and RINSE both at 56.0 s nominal load and DRY at 36.0 s, and a zero blocking-to-processing ratio. The measured schedule is far above the 56.0 s nominal lower bound: 199.0 s, about 3.55x the nominal minimum load. Chamber processing seconds match the nominal loads exactly (CLEAN 56.0, RINSE 56.0, DRY 36.0), so the gap is not extra processing; it is waiting and blocking. Robot action time is 151.0 s with robot utilization 0.3775, mean chamber utilization only 0.1233, and total chamber blocking 78.0 s (CLEAN 25.0, RINSE 24.0, DRY 29.0). Wafer waiting is large and grows monotonically by release order: W01 12.0 s, W02 40.0 s, W03 106.0 s, W04 131.0 s, average 72.25 s. This pattern indicates the bottleneck rule policy serializes the batch poorly: later wafers wait progressively longer, so the schedule is dominated by robot transfer serialization and chamber blocking rather than by chamber capacity. The static blocking-to-processing ratio of 0.0 was not predictive of the dynamic behavior; the measured blocking_rate is 0.065 and blocking is substantial. This is consistent with the F_competition finding that makespan there was dominated by robot serialization and blocking rather than raw chamber load. It also falsifies the optimistic branch of the prior hypothesis that a close-to-nominal baseline would indicate chamber capacity dominates; instead the baseline is far above nominal, indicating robot serialization dominates on G_dual_bottleneck as well under this rule. Cost was negligible: about 0.41 s wall and 0.046 s skill time, so this was a high-value probe. The next question is whether the 199.0 s baseline is near the best achievable or whether a stronger method can substantially reduce it. CP-SAT is the natural next test because it can optimize transfer ordering and blocking jointly, and the untried CP budget for this scenario is 1 s (estimated about 4 s wall), which is cheap relative to the remaining wall budget of about 547 s and the remaining CP search budget of 56 s. A 1 s CP run tests whether the rule baseline is far from optimal and whether the solver can find a verified complete-batch schedule below 199.0 s. If CP-SAT at 1 s already improves substantially, that would indicate the rule policy is the limiting factor and that further CP budget is worth spending; if it does not improve, that would suggest the instance is hard for the current encoding or that 199.0 s is closer to the practical limit for these methods.",
  "failure_explanation": "Round-005 did not fail verification: the simulation completed, all four wafers were released and completed, independent replay was valid, and there were zero constraint violations and zero deadlocks. It was accepted as the first verified candidate for G_dual_bottleneck. The only sense in which it underperformed is relative to the static nominal lower bound: the verified makespan of 199.0 s is about 3.55x the 56.0 s nominal minimum load, and the prior hypothesis anticipated that a baseline close to nominal would indicate chamber capacity dominates. That branch was falsified. The likely cause is that the bottleneck rule policy does not coordinate robot transfers well on this dual-bottleneck instance, producing 78.0 s of chamber blocking and monotonically increasing wafer waiting (12.0, 40.0, 106.0, 131.0 s), which indicates serialization and starvation of later wafers rather than chamber capacity limits. The static blocking-to-processing ratio of 0.0 was not a reliable predictor of dynamic blocking. This is a meaningful negative result about the rule policy, not about the simulator or verification.",
  "testable_hypothesis": "On G_dual_bottleneck, a CP-SAT run with max_seconds=1 will return a verified complete-batch Makespan strictly below the 199.0 s rule baseline, because CP-SAT can jointly optimize transfer ordering and chamber blocking, whereas the bottleneck rule policy serializes transfers and leaves later wafers waiting (W04 131.0 s). If CP-SAT at 1 s does not improve on 199.0 s, that would indicate the instance is hard for the current encoding or that the rule baseline is already near the practical limit for these methods; if it improves substantially, further CP budget is justified.",
  "next_proposal": {
    "action": "experiment",
    "scenario_id": "G_dual_bottleneck",
    "method": "cpsat",
    "parameters": {
      "max_seconds": 1
    },
    "rationale": "G_dual_bottleneck now has a verified rule baseline of 199.0 s, which is about 3.55x the 56.0 s nominal lower bound, with 78.0 s of chamber blocking and monotonically increasing wafer waiting, indicating robot serialization dominates under the rule policy. CP-SAT is the cheapest available method that can jointly optimize transfer ordering and blocking, and the untried CP budget for this scenario is 1 s (estimated about 4 s wall), well within the remaining wall budget of about 547 s and the remaining CP search budget of 56 s. This tests whether the rule baseline is far from optimal and whether the solver can find a verified complete-batch schedule below 199.0 s, which would justify further CP budget or a different method.",
    "hypothesis": "CP-SAT with max_seconds=1 on G_dual_bottleneck will return a verified complete-batch Makespan strictly below the 199.0 s rule baseline, with lower chamber blocking and lower average wafer waiting than the rule schedule, because it can jointly optimize transfer ordering and blocking.",
    "expected_effect": "A verified complete-batch Makespan for G_dual_bottleneck below 199.0 s, or a non-improving result that would indicate the rule baseline is near the practical limit for these methods. Either outcome informs whether to spend more CP budget or try RL on this scenario.",
    "evidence": [
      "round-005"
    ]
  }
}

{
  "explanation": "Round-006 tested the prior hypothesis that CP-SAT with max_seconds=1 would beat the 199.0 s bottleneck-rule baseline on G_dual_bottleneck. The hypothesis was confirmed: the run returned a verified complete-batch Makespan of 183.0 s, a 16.0 s (about 8.0%) improvement, and was accepted as the new incumbent. Verification is strong: status completed, 4 of 4 wafers released and completed, zero unfinished wafers, independent replay valid, zero constraint violations, zero deadlocks, and the CP model's predicted makespan (183.0 s) exactly matched the original-Simulator event-integral makespan. The solver reported FEASIBLE with objective 183.0 s and best bound 142.0 s, an absolute gap of 41.0 s (relative gap about 0.224), so optimality is not established; 183.0 s is an incumbent, not an optimum. The improvement came from a different schedule structure rather than from more processing: chamber processing seconds are unchanged (CLEAN 56.0, RINSE 56.0, DRY 36.0), and robot action seconds are unchanged at 151.0 s. What changed is the waiting/blocking distribution. Total chamber blocking rose slightly from 78.0 s to 88.0 s (CLEAN 19.0, RINSE 34.0, DRY 35.0), yet makespan fell by 16.0 s. Wafer waiting was redistributed rather than reduced in aggregate: average waiting fell only slightly from 72.25 s to 69.75 s, but the ordering inverted, with W01 waiting 121.0 s and W04 only 14.0 s, versus the rule schedule where W01 waited 12.0 s and W04 waited 131.0 s. This indicates CP-SAT prioritized earlier-released wafers and accepted more blocking on some chambers to reduce the critical-path tail, rather than simply minimizing total blocking. The static nominal lower bound of 56.0 s remains far below the incumbent (183.0 s is about 3.27x nominal), and the solver's own bound of 142.0 s is still about 2.54x nominal, so the instance remains dominated by robot serialization and blocking rather than chamber capacity. The prior reflection's falsified branch (that a near-nominal rule baseline would indicate capacity dominance) remains falsified. Cost was low: about 1.96 s wall and 1.54 s skill time, a high-value probe. The open question is whether the 41.0 s solver gap can be closed with more CP search budget, or whether the encoding itself limits improvement. The next untried CP budget for this scenario is 3 s, which is cheap and directly tests whether additional search time converts the FEASIBLE incumbent toward the 142.0 s bound.",
  "failure_explanation": "Round-006 did not fail verification: the simulation completed, all four wafers were released and completed, independent replay was valid, and there were zero constraint violations and zero deadlocks. It was accepted as a lower-makespan incumbent. The only shortfall is optimality: the solver returned FEASIBLE, not OPTIMAL, with a 41.0 s absolute gap to its own 142.0 s bound, so 183.0 s is not proven optimal. The prior hypothesis that CP-SAT at 1 s would beat 199.0 s was confirmed, so no branch of that hypothesis was falsified. The remaining uncertainty is whether the 41.0 s gap reflects insufficient search time or a limitation of the current encoding; the 1 s budget allowed only 27743 branches and 1770 conflicts, which is modest, so insufficient search time is the more likely explanation and is directly testable with a larger CP budget.",
  "testable_hypothesis": "On G_dual_bottleneck, a CP-SAT run with max_seconds=3 will return a verified complete-batch Makespan strictly below the 183.0 s incumbent, because the 1 s run left a 41.0 s gap to its own 142.0 s bound and only explored 27743 branches, so additional search time should find a better transfer ordering. If CP-SAT at 3 s does not improve on 183.0 s, that would indicate the current encoding or the instance's robot-serialization structure limits further gains at this budget, and would justify either a larger CP budget or a different method such as RL.",
  "next_proposal": {
    "action": "experiment",
    "scenario_id": "G_dual_bottleneck",
    "method": "cpsat",
    "parameters": {
      "max_seconds": 3
    },
    "rationale": "G_dual_bottleneck now has a verified CP-SAT incumbent of 183.0 s, improved from the 199.0 s rule baseline, but the solver returned FEASIBLE with a 41.0 s gap to its own 142.0 s bound after only 1 s of search (27743 branches, 1770 conflicts). The next untried CP budget for this scenario is 3 s, which is cheap relative to the remaining wall budget and the remaining CP search budget, and directly tests whether additional search time closes the gap. This is the most informative next step because it isolates search-time effects on the same encoding before considering a different method.",
    "hypothesis": "CP-SAT with max_seconds=3 on G_dual_bottleneck will return a verified complete-batch Makespan strictly below the 183.0 s incumbent, with a smaller absolute gap to the solver bound than the 1 s run, because the 1 s run was search-limited rather than encoding-limited.",
    "expected_effect": "A verified complete-batch Makespan for G_dual_bottleneck below 183.0 s, or a non-improving result indicating the encoding or instance structure limits gains at this budget. Either outcome informs whether to spend a larger CP budget or switch to RL on this scenario.",
    "evidence": [
      "round-005",
      "round-006"
    ]
  }
}

{
  "explanation": "Round-007 tested the prior hypothesis that CP-SAT with max_seconds=3 on G_dual_bottleneck would return a verified complete-batch Makespan strictly below the 183.0 s incumbent, because the 1 s run appeared search-limited (41.0 s gap to its own 142.0 s bound). The hypothesis was falsified: the 3 s run returned exactly the same verified Makespan of 183.0 s, with the same solver bound of 142.0 s and the same 41.0 s absolute gap (relative gap about 0.224). Verification remains strong: status completed, 4 of 4 wafers released and completed, zero unfinished wafers, independent replay valid, zero constraint violations, zero deadlocks, and the CP model's predicted makespan (183.0 s) exactly matched the original-Simulator event-integral makespan. The schedule structure is also identical to round-006: chamber processing seconds unchanged (CLEAN 56.0, RINSE 56.0, DRY 36.0), robot action seconds unchanged at 151.0 s, chamber blocking unchanged (CLEAN 19.0, RINSE 34.0, DRY 35.0, total 88.0 s), and per-wafer waiting unchanged (W01 121.0, W02 94.0, W03 50.0, W04 14.0). Tripling the search budget from 1 s to 3 s roughly doubled the search effort (branches 27743 to 54762, conflicts 1770 to 8596) yet produced no change in the incumbent or the bound. This is evidence that the 1 s run was not merely search-limited in a way that more time at this scale would fix; the same incumbent is reached quickly and additional time at 3 s does not move either the incumbent or the dual bound. The static nominal lower bound of 56.0 s remains far below the incumbent (183.0 s is about 3.27x nominal), and the solver's own bound of 142.0 s is still about 2.54x nominal, so the instance remains dominated by robot serialization and blocking rather than chamber capacity. Cost was low: about 4.44 s wall and 3.60 s skill time, a cheap probe. The open question is whether the 41.0 s gap reflects a limitation of the current encoding or the instance's robot-serialization structure, or whether a larger CP budget (10 s or 30 s) would close it. Because 3 s produced no movement at all, the marginal value of another small CP increment is questionable; a larger budget or a different method (RL) is the more informative next probe. The next untried CP budget for this scenario is 10 s, which is cheap relative to the remaining wall budget and remaining CP search budget, and directly tests whether a substantially larger search budget converts the FEASIBLE incumbent toward the 142.0 s bound.",
  "failure_explanation": "Round-007 did not fail verification: the simulation completed, all four wafers were released and completed, independent replay was valid, and there were zero constraint violations and zero deadlocks. It was not accepted because it produced no lexicographic improvement over the 183.0 s incumbent. The prior hypothesis that CP-SAT at 3 s would strictly improve on 183.0 s was falsified: the 3 s run returned the identical Makespan of 183.0 s, the identical solver bound of 142.0 s, and the identical 41.0 s gap. Search effort roughly doubled (branches 27743 to 54762, conflicts 1770 to 8596) with no change in incumbent or bound, which weakens the earlier explanation that the 1 s run was simply search-limited at this scale. The remaining uncertainty is whether the 41.0 s gap reflects insufficient search time at larger budgets, a limitation of the current encoding, or the instance's robot-serialization structure. The 3 s result suggests that small CP increments are unlikely to help, so the next test should use a substantially larger CP budget or a different method.",
  "testable_hypothesis": "On G_dual_bottleneck, a CP-SAT run with max_seconds=10 will return a verified complete-batch Makespan strictly below the 183.0 s incumbent, because the 3 s run reached the same incumbent and bound as the 1 s run despite roughly doubling search effort, so a substantially larger budget is needed to test whether the 41.0 s gap to the 142.0 s bound is search-limited at all. If CP-SAT at 10 s does not improve on 183.0 s, that would indicate the current encoding or the instance's robot-serialization structure limits further gains at this budget, and would justify either a 30 s CP budget or a different method such as RL.",
  "next_proposal": {
    "action": "experiment",
    "scenario_id": "G_dual_bottleneck",
    "method": "cpsat",
    "parameters": {
